---

A **held region** is a component that the server selects at runtime and the client renders. The props are type-checked and go over the wire. It is the ogygia version of RSC: the server decides the UI, and the client only paints it.

::: tip When to use
Use a held region when the *selection of the component* belongs to the server. Examples: search results that are a card or an empty-state, a feed with mixed item types, a dashboard tile that the backend decides. A [server island](/docs/regions/server-islands) fills a *fixed* hole with request-specific HTML. A held region lets the **server select the component** that fills it. The client never imports the options.
:::

```svelte
<script>
  import { region, Region } from 'ogygia';
  import Card from './Card.svelte';
</script>

<!-- inline: rendered in this SSR pass -->
<Region of={region(Card, { id })} />
```

## Deferred (held) regions

Mark the import, and the component becomes a **signed capability**. The server makes the capability in a load or a remote function. The client fetches it, swaps the HTML in, and hydrates. The client never imports the component. There are two markers:

- `with { wake: 'load' }` bakes an interactive schedule. `region()` wakes it on that timing.
- `with { region: 'raw' }` bakes **no** schedule. The result is HTML only, unless the `region()` call sets a schedule.

```ts title=search.remote.ts
// server picks the component
import PackageCard from './PackageCard.svelte' with { wake: 'load' };
import EmptyResult from './EmptyResult.svelte' with { region: 'raw' };
import { query } from '$app/server';
import { region } from 'ogygia';

export const search = query(v.string(), async (raw) => {
  const hit = DB[raw.toLowerCase()];
  // PackageCard bakes wake:'load' → interactive; EmptyResult is HTML only.
  return hit ? region(PackageCard, hit) : region(EmptyResult, { query: raw });
});
```

```svelte
<!-- the client renders whatever it receives -->
<script>
  import { Region } from 'ogygia';
  import { search } from './search.remote';
  let result = $state(null);
</script>

<button onclick={async () => (result = await search(q))}>Search</button>
{#if result}<Region of={result} />{/if}
```

## Where the schedule comes from

The wake schedule of a held region is **baked at the import** with a `wake:` mark. The vocabulary is the same as for a placed island:

| Mark | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `wake: 'load'` | interactive, hydrate on load |
| `wake: 'idle'` | interactive, hydrate when idle |
| `wake: 'visible'` | interactive, hydrate when scrolled into view |
| `wake: '(min-width: 60rem)'` | interactive, hydrate when the media query matches |
| `region: 'raw'` | HTML only, no baked schedule |

A held region has no `render` mark of its own. You decide *when to fetch* through the moment you render `<Region>`. The baked `wake:` mark, or the schedule at the `region()` call, decides *when it wakes*.

`region: 'raw'` is the escape hatch. It bakes nothing. Thus the `region()` call sets the schedule with a `(data) => options` function. This is applicable to a registry of raw components. Each component then decides its timing from its own data:

```ts
region(block, data, (d) => ({ wake: d.interactive ? 'load' : undefined }));
```

## Mountable `.ts` bindings

A `.ts` binding with `with { wake: … }` is a real component. It is not only a value for `region()`. Thus a **third-party renderer** can place it directly and get the `<ogygia-region>` shell. An example is a page builder, such as Builder.io, that renders a registered component with `<svelte:component this={component}/>`. Its JS loads only when two conditions are true: the component is placed, and its wake schedule fires. The binding also stays crossable over the wire through `region()`. The region metadata rides on the binding. One shape does both jobs:

```ts title=registry.ts
import Widget from './Widget.svelte' with { wake: 'visible' };

export const registry = [
  { name: 'widget', component: Widget }, // Builder places it via <svelte:component>; wakes on scroll
];
```

For a **named or barrel** import, which `with { … }` cannot reach, use the [`asRegion`](/docs/macros/asregion) macro. It makes the same mountable binding:

```ts
import { Header } from '@company/ui';
const HeaderRegion = import.meta.og.asRegion(Header, { wake: 'visible' });
```

`with { region: 'raw' }` stays a bare descriptor. It renders only through `region()`, not by direct placement.

## Try it

The demo below is a search box. On submit, it calls a remote. The remote finds the query and **selects a component on the server**: an interactive `PackageCard` for a hit, a static `EmptyResult` for a miss. It signs the result and returns it. This page never imports the result components. Search `svelte`, `kit`, `vite`, or `ogygia`. All other queries return the static card.

<script>
  import SearchDemo from './SearchDemo.svelte' with { wake: 'load' };
  import SingleFlightDemo from './SingleFlightDemo.svelte' with { wake: 'load' };
</script>

<div class="doc-demo">
  <SearchDemo />
</div>

The star button of the card shows that it hydrated. The "no match" result ships as pure HTML. Look at the page source: there is no JS chunk for it.

## The region owns the wait

One rule: **the loading UI of a region lives on the region.** A held-region fetch has two async phases, and they end at different times. First, the request: your remote call resolves. Second, the paint: the HTML lands, and its stylesheet loads. Only the region sees both phases. A `loading` flag, an `{#await}`, or a boundary next to the call sees only the first phase. Thus it says "done" before the card is on screen.

So do not track the wait at all. `of` accepts the **promise** directly from the remote call. Then the region owns the full wait. Its `placeholder` snippet is the one loading indicator, from start to end:

```svelte
<button type="submit">Search</button>   <!-- no loading state to reconcile -->

{#if query}
  <Region of={search(query)}>
    {#snippet placeholder()}<p class="hint">Searching…</p>{/snippet}
  </Region>
{:else}
  <p class="hint">Search to fetch a component from the server.</p>
{/if}
```

The timeline on a slow link, from submit:

| Time | Phase | What's on screen |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `0 ms` | request in flight | your `Searching…` placeholder |
| request resolves | HTML here; CSS `<link>` hoisted to `<head>`, sheet downloading | still the placeholder |
| CSS `load` | region paints | the **styled** card, in one step |

There is no blank gap: the placeholder holds the space until the paint. There is no unstyled flash: the paint waits for the sheet. Nothing outside the region says when the region is ready. Thus nothing can disagree. ogygia limits the stylesheet wait to a few seconds. A broken sheet then paints, and does not stop the page.

A **new search** gives `of` a new promise. The region keeps the *previous* card on screen until the next result has its styles. Then it morphs in place. There is no placeholder flash between results.

Why a held region has this window at all: it carries **its own CSS**. The page never imported `PackageCard`. Thus its styles are on no page stylesheet. They travel with the region. A held region rendered in the *server* pass (a blocks page, SDUI) has no window: its links go into `<head>` during SSR, and it arrives with its styles. No placeholder is involved.

::: warning Why `placeholder`, not `ogygiaFallback`
`ogygiaFallback` is the reserved slot for a `render: 'deferred'` server island (a fixed hole). A held region is a *value* that you place. Thus its not-ready UI is a snippet on its `<Region>`. That is plain Svelte.
:::

### Don't await it yourself

With `experimental.async` on, you *can* write `of={await search(query)}` inside a `<svelte:boundary>` and let its `pending` snippet control the wait. Do not do this. The boundary stops its wait when the fetch resolves. But the region still has a paint to do: swap the HTML, load its stylesheet. You then own a wait that you cannot fully see. Your "done" signal fires early.

The runtime still protects you. A region **never paints without its styles**. It holds its previous content, or its `placeholder`, or empty space, until the sheet is in. Thus the failure mode is a hold with nothing better to show, not a flash. But the correct fix is to not make the gap. Give the region the promise. Put the loading UI where the lifecycle lives. Boundaries, `{#await}`, and loading flags stay good for *your* UI around the region. Region readiness is not theirs to report.

## Crossing shapes: the wire law

All examples above send *one* region. The general rule for many:

> **A region is the only unit of code that crosses. Everything else is data. Any data shape crosses if its leaves are regions.**

Arrays, records, nesting: the transport does not care. It walks the value. It makes one signed capability for each region leaf that it finds, in each position:

```ts
// a mixed list — each leaf its own ticket
return hits.map((h) => (h.sponsored ? region(Ad, h) : region(Card, h)));

// named slots — mix eager and lazy freely
return { header: region(Head, u), main: await region(Chart, o), rail: region(Rail, u) };

// a region as a PROP of another region — the leaf decides where it renders
return await region(FeedCard, { author, media: region(VideoEmbed, { src }) });
```

### What `await` does

`await region(…)` renders the component on the server. It bakes that HTML into the ticket. Thus the region paints when the response lands, with no extra request. A **bare** `return region(…)` from an async remote does the same. The language awaits a thenable at `return`. Thus all values returned from a remote cross with their HTML baked.

Leaves nested inside a container are the exception. Nothing awaits them. Thus they cross as a plain address, and the client fetches each hole on mount. That is not a knob you turn. It follows from where the region sits. Timing is never the job of this axis. Timing is [`wake`](/docs/regions/client-islands).

#### Recipe: a lazy top-level return

Sometimes one returned region must stay a plain address. Then a CDN can **cache its endpoint across visitors**, and no per-request HTML is baked into the response. There is no library helper for this, by intent. It is one line that you own. A region is an object, and its eager `then` is non-enumerable. Thus a spread drops it:

```ts
const lazy = <R>(r: R): R => ({ ...r });   // strips the auto-await; keep it in your codebase

export const card = query(v.string(), async (id) => lazy(region(Card, { id })));
// now `card()` ships a reference; the client fetches /🏝️?… which a CDN can cache with a maxAge preset
```

Use this rarely. For a hole shared across visitors, a placed [server island](/docs/regions/server-islands) is usually the cleaner tool. It is endpoint-fetched and cacheable, and it does not touch the eager/lazy seam.

### Composition: the recomposer

Sometimes the shape needs interpretation: a tree, a feed, a layout. Then ship a normal marked component. It takes the shape as props and changes it back into markup:

```ts
import FeedList from '$lib/FeedList.svelte' with { region: 'raw' };
return await region(FeedList, { items: hits.map((h) => region(cardFor(h), h)) });
```

`FeedList` renders `{#each items as it}<Region of={it} />{/each}` plus its chrome. With `await`, the full composition renders in one server pass: one HTML payload with the stylesheet of each leaf. Nested interactive leaves are self-describing islands. They wake on their own schedules after they land. This operates recursively (trees with `children`). Thus a full blocks-style page can cross as one value.

## Live regions

`query.live` can send a held region again and again over time. `yield` a held region on each tick. Its rendered, signed HTML rides the channel you already have. There is no per-tick fetch:

```ts
export const dashboard = query.live(v.string(), async function* (id) {
  for await (const stats of feed(id)) yield region(StatCard, { stats });
});
```

`<Region of={dashboard(id).current} />` swaps the first tick in. After that, a raw (HTML-only, `region: 'raw'`) held region **morphs** in place. Focus and typed text stay. An interactive one is **kept alive**: its state stays, and it does not hydrate again. One slot always shows the latest tick.

## Single-flight mutations

A `command` that returns a held region sends the re-rendered HTML back in its **own** response. A mounted `<Region>` at that address updates from it. There is no follow-up fetch. One round trip both mutates and repaints:

```ts
export const bump = command(v.string(), async (id) => {
  await db.increment(id);
  return region(Counter, await db.get(id));  // the fresh region rides the command's response
});
```

Call `bump(id)` from an island. The on-page region at that address repaints from the response of the command. There is no extra GET to the endpoint.

<div class="doc-demo">
  <SingleFlightDemo />
</div>

## Warm a hole early: preload(region)

`preload(region)` warms the HTML of one hole before its schedule. Thus the hole can paint early, while its own `wake` stays lazy. Import it from `ogygia`. It takes a region value and nothing else.

```ts
import { preload } from 'ogygia';
preload(region(Recommendations, { userId }));
```

## Do / don't

- **Do** use a held region when the server owns the *which-component* decision. Use a [server island](/docs/regions/server-islands) when the component is fixed.
- **Do** select a raw held region (`with { region: 'raw' }`) for result UI that never needs interaction. It ships zero JS.
- **Don't** import the result components on the client. The server sends them. The client only renders `<Region>`.


